WEBVTT

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:02.650
And listened to my story about a man named Jed

00:00:02.650 --> 00:00:05.250
A poor mountaineer barely kept his family fed

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.130
And then one day he was shooting at some food

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.930
And up through the ground come a bubbling crude

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:15.260
Oil, that is, black gold, Texas tea

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:18.740
Well, the first thing you know, old Jed's a millionaire

00:00:18.740 --> 00:00:21.720
The kinfolk said, Jed, move away from there

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:24.190
Said, California is the place you ought to be

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:26.650
So they loaded up the truck and they moved to Beverly

00:00:26.650 --> 00:00:30.000
Hills, that is, swimming pools

00:00:30.380 --> 00:00:36.600
movie stars the Beverly Hillbillies

00:01:16.540 --> 00:01:19.780
- This ought to get the clamberts interested in deep sea fishing, don't you agree?

00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:22.880
- It is a masterstroke of psychological strategy.

00:01:22.880 --> 00:01:24.340
- Thanks. Grab the tail.

00:01:24.340 --> 00:01:27.650
- It cleverly takes cognizance of their innate love of the sport of hunting,

00:01:27.650 --> 00:01:30.000
which has been drastically curtailed by Ellie Mae's love

00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:31.830
- Of the very creatures they hunt.

00:01:31.830 --> 00:01:32.970
- Yeah, grab the tail.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.390
- It provides these active rugged mountaineers

00:01:35.390 --> 00:01:37.810
with all the thrills and excitement of a chase

00:01:37.810 --> 00:01:39.930
they so thoroughly enjoy.

00:01:39.930 --> 00:01:40.830
- Yeah, grab the tail.

00:01:40.830 --> 00:01:43.380
- And so they become devotees.

00:01:43.380 --> 00:01:44.960
It will prevent their leaving Beverly Hills

00:01:44.960 --> 00:01:46.460
and returning to their home.

00:01:46.460 --> 00:01:49.220
For, by its very nature, big game fishing

00:01:49.220 --> 00:01:52.400
is impossible in their landlocked mountainous terrain.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.350
I repeat, chief.

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.640
It is a master stroke of psychological strategy.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.000
Hmm.

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:16.170
because I said three lousy words don't you agree I'm gonna say three more you

00:02:16.170 --> 00:02:20.500
know dook is plum tuckered out we walk clean over to the golf pasture looking

00:02:20.500 --> 00:02:30.000
for some game we got mean mouth and stick chased out of there

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:34.220
- Well, we did manage to find a few of them golf bags.

00:02:37.180 --> 00:02:38.160
- Throw 'em out, Jed.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:39.980
They just can't be cooked.

00:02:39.980 --> 00:02:42.980
I've boiled 'em and porched 'em and fried 'em.

00:02:43.980 --> 00:02:46.840
And they still come out gummy as an old boot.

00:02:46.840 --> 00:02:49.840
Oh, Bungler, I wanted some fresh game meat.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.840
- Well, looks like we's caught betwixt in between, Granny.

00:02:52.840 --> 00:02:54.840
Folks don't want me shooting a gun,

00:02:54.840 --> 00:02:56.840
and when I snare the game, Ellie makes a pet out of it.

00:02:56.840 --> 00:02:58.700
- Uncle Jed! Granny!

00:02:58.700 --> 00:02:59.030
All right.

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:02.180
- Come see with Mr. Drysdale and Miss Jane Bruggers.

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:04.490
A great big old fish about this long.

00:03:04.490 --> 00:03:06.930
- Fish that size must be a channel cat.

00:03:06.930 --> 00:03:10.430
- Yeah, some catfish, rolling cornmeal, deep fried.

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:11.990
Oh, it tastes pretty good right now, huh?

00:03:11.990 --> 00:03:13.110
- Sure.

00:03:13.110 --> 00:03:14.110
Come on, let's go.

00:03:15.250 --> 00:03:16.510
- Remember, not a word to my wife

00:03:16.510 --> 00:03:18.130
about giving this trophy to the Clampetts.

00:03:18.130 --> 00:03:18.820
- Right, Chief.

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:20.560
- That fishing trip to the Gulf of Mexico

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:22.400
cost us about $4,000.

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:24.000
And this is all we have to show for it.

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:25.150
- I understand.

00:03:25.150 --> 00:03:29.480
- Plus which, it took her six hours to land it.

00:03:30.480 --> 00:03:31.780
Louise, that is a big fish.

00:03:31.780 --> 00:03:33.300
Ain't no catfish.

00:03:33.300 --> 00:03:34.300
Looks like a carp.

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:35.800
No, no, this is a tarpon.

00:03:35.800 --> 00:03:37.300
That's a saltwater fish.

00:03:37.300 --> 00:03:39.300
Oh, you cook 'em in saltwater, do you?

00:03:39.300 --> 00:03:41.300
No, no, it lives in saltwater.

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:43.300
The tarpon is a noted deep-sea game fish.

00:03:43.300 --> 00:03:45.810
And I want you to have it for your game room.

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:47.310
A what room?

00:03:47.310 --> 00:03:48.580
Game room.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.580
That's the one with the trophies on the wall.

00:03:50.580 --> 00:03:51.580
The billiard room.

00:03:51.580 --> 00:03:53.580
Oh, you mean a fancy eating room.

00:03:53.580 --> 00:03:55.580
Yes, as you so quaintly call it.

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:56.580
What do I have to do to him?

00:03:56.580 --> 00:03:57.980
Nothing.

00:03:57.980 --> 00:03:59.990
It's been prepared by an expert taxidermist.

00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:02.060
- Feels nice and crisp.

00:04:02.060 --> 00:04:05.120
- I'd sure like to see the skillet he was fried in.

00:04:06.260 --> 00:04:07.100
- Me too.

00:04:07.100 --> 00:04:08.320
- It feels like the grease got too hot

00:04:08.320 --> 00:04:09.260
and he curled up a match.

00:04:09.260 --> 00:04:11.600
- No, no, no, he's not fried.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:12.440
- Baked?

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:15.000
- No, no, no, don't smell like he's been smoked.

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:16.440
- Turpin are rarely cooked at all.

00:04:16.440 --> 00:04:18.830
Once caught, they're usually preserved, like this one.

00:04:18.830 --> 00:04:20.790
- Oh, preserved.

00:04:20.790 --> 00:04:22.470
- Like pickled?

00:04:22.470 --> 00:04:24.170
- Well, yes, in a way.

00:04:24.170 --> 00:04:25.770
It's quite a process.

00:04:25.770 --> 00:04:28.270
They're clean, scraped, treated with chemicals,

00:04:28.270 --> 00:04:29.760
even the bones are removed.

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:37.180
it's made and this miss hathaway mr claver i'm sure you'd enjoy catching a fish like this i would

00:04:37.180 --> 00:04:41.760
for a fact now there's a critter even ellie wouldn't make a pit hour then it's all settled

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:46.880
now i'm going out and get you the finest deep sea fishing equipment that money can box oh no need

00:04:46.880 --> 00:04:51.680
to do that mr drysdale i can cut myself a pole make a hook out of a bent nail no catching big

00:04:51.680 --> 00:04:56.490
game fish like tarpon and marlin requires very special equipment it does indeed mr clavin

00:04:56.490 --> 00:05:00.000
the black marlin for example which is a native of our own pacific borders

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:05.030
No, fetch this fish out to the kitchen.

00:05:05.250 --> 00:05:06.870
I gotta figure out how to cook him.

00:05:07.250 --> 00:05:09.710
But, Granny, Miss Jane said you didn't have to do nothing more to it.

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:10.460
She said that it was all...

00:05:10.460 --> 00:05:11.980
I heard what she said.

00:05:12.320 --> 00:05:15.820
But I say that this fish ain't got a fresh-caught look to it.

00:05:16.060 --> 00:05:18.440
And nobody's chomping on him till I've cooked him.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:20.340
Yes, ma'am, Granny.

00:05:32.300 --> 00:05:34.000
She's right. It could use some tendering up.

00:05:35.480 --> 00:05:39.300
You gonna commence a tendering up, you wall-eyed ocean-going farming?

00:05:39.640 --> 00:05:40.800
You cooking that fish?

00:05:40.900 --> 00:05:42.360
I ain't giving him a bath.

00:05:42.900 --> 00:05:46.680
Gene said he already been prepared by experts somewhere other...

00:05:46.680 --> 00:05:47.640
Taxi driver.

00:05:48.120 --> 00:05:49.280
That sounds like it.

00:05:50.100 --> 00:05:52.620
He better stick to driving taxis.

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:54.780
He don't know shucks about fish.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:57.780
I've been boiling this rascal for two hours.

00:05:58.140 --> 00:06:00.000
And he's still tough as harness.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:01.940
leather. You know what it could

00:06:01.940 --> 00:06:04.140
be, Granny? Why? Swimming around in the

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:06.120
salt water all them years is what toughened

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:08.010
him up. You know what Brian will do

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:10.070
to any kind of hide. I know what it's done

00:06:10.070 --> 00:06:12.050
to his hide. Why, I've dove

00:06:12.050 --> 00:06:13.810
three knives trying to cut him up.

00:06:14.050 --> 00:06:15.890
I finally had Jethro fetch him out here and

00:06:15.890 --> 00:06:17.450
throw him into my big kettle hole.

00:06:18.080 --> 00:06:19.940
And cooking him two hours ain't softened him up none?

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:21.680
Not one smidgen.

00:06:22.000 --> 00:06:24.050
And I've had both ends in the water.

00:06:26.050 --> 00:06:28.190
Granny, we's hungry. Ain't that fish ready yet?

00:06:28.290 --> 00:06:30.000
That fish ain't never gonna be

00:06:30.830 --> 00:06:32.110
let's go catch one of our own.

00:06:32.490 --> 00:06:33.910
Yeah, with this fish-winding machine.

00:06:34.490 --> 00:06:36.530
It'd be a freshwater fish if I cook it.

00:06:36.790 --> 00:06:39.410
I'd just leave trying to stew in alligators, that thing.

00:06:39.850 --> 00:06:41.550
Come on, Jethro, let's dig some worms.

00:06:41.920 --> 00:06:42.660
Now, hold on.

00:06:43.320 --> 00:06:45.900
I reckon it'd hurt Mr. Drydale's feelings considerable

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:47.940
if he was to find out we didn't eat his fish.

00:06:48.560 --> 00:06:51.140
But Uncle Jed, I pretty near broke a tooth on that thing.

00:06:51.560 --> 00:06:54.780
And if Jethro can't bite through it, nobody can.

00:06:55.740 --> 00:06:57.160
How about resting my cat?

00:06:57.520 --> 00:07:00.000
He's had his eye on it ever since Jethro fetched it out here.

00:07:00.570 --> 00:07:02.230
- Yeah, let's give it to him.

00:07:02.230 --> 00:07:04.610
- It's a heap of fish for one cat.

00:07:04.610 --> 00:07:05.850
- Don't worry about that, Granny.

00:07:05.850 --> 00:07:16.180
Pears like Rusty is rounding him up some friends to help.

00:07:16.180 --> 00:07:22.720
- All I can say to you is, loads of luck.

00:07:37.040 --> 00:07:38.540
- Are those for Paul and Granny?

00:07:38.540 --> 00:07:40.160
- Granny's gonna use the new store-bought pole

00:07:40.160 --> 00:07:41.280
from Mr. Drysdale,

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:43.410
and Uncle Jed's gonna try out his new fishing machine.

00:07:44.910 --> 00:07:46.770
- You dig a nice bucket of worms, Ellie?

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:48.760
- Yes, sir, Paul, that's on a truck.

00:07:48.760 --> 00:07:50.480
What's that thing you're wearing?

00:07:50.480 --> 00:07:52.780
- Well, this here is a fishing machine harness

00:07:52.780 --> 00:07:54.360
for holding this here fishing machine.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:57.330
- I still say that's a mighty short pole.

00:07:57.330 --> 00:07:58.730
- I reckon it's meant for fishing

00:07:58.730 --> 00:08:00.000
over the side of a boat, Granny.

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:01.520
Ain't gonna get me on no boat.

00:08:01.660 --> 00:08:02.480
Ain't figuring to.

00:08:02.900 --> 00:08:04.260
They worked as well off a bridge.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:06.200
You know any good fishing bridges, Jethro?

00:08:07.040 --> 00:08:09.280
Only bridge I know goes over the Los Angeles River.

00:08:10.160 --> 00:08:12.220
Ain't nothing like a river for catfish.

00:08:12.400 --> 00:08:13.160
Let's get going.

00:08:13.460 --> 00:08:15.240
Wasn't much water in it last time we was there.

00:08:15.380 --> 00:08:17.100
Maybe the beavers had it dammed up.

00:08:17.440 --> 00:08:18.460
Isn't they still there?

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:19.760
Can I bring home a beaver?

00:08:20.160 --> 00:08:21.620
No more critters.

00:08:22.060 --> 00:08:23.000
Quiet down, Granny.

00:08:23.060 --> 00:08:24.400
We ain't bringing home nothing but fish.

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:25.420
Let her roll, Jethro.

00:08:27.280 --> 00:08:28.420
Let's go.

00:08:36.010 --> 00:08:38.800
Can't throw your wasting time, there ain't no fish in there.

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:42.300
- That is the poorest excuse of a river I ever did see.

00:08:42.300 --> 00:08:44.400
- Pitiful, just pitiful.

00:08:45.780 --> 00:08:47.640
- Los Angeles is so ashamed of that river,

00:08:47.640 --> 00:08:50.400
they had to put up cement walls to hide it.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.700
- Maybe it'd be better when the spring thaw sets in.

00:08:54.700 --> 00:08:57.360
Let's go find this crick or pond.

00:08:57.360 --> 00:08:59.410
- Wait a minute, I got a bite.

00:09:00.570 --> 00:09:01.990
My doggies, he has a gut.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:09.860
What fur?

00:09:10.180 --> 00:09:11.950
This ain't nothing but a boot.

00:09:12.050 --> 00:09:12.710
I know it.

00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:14.170
I'm going to fish for the other one.

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:15.710
They're better than the ones I'm wearing.

00:09:20.110 --> 00:09:23.740
Let's wait here.

00:09:24.020 --> 00:09:25.900
Granny and me is going in and talking to Mr. Drydale.

00:09:26.120 --> 00:09:29.050
Yeah, I have a few things to say that you might oughtn't to hear.

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:30.000
Him and him.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:34.610
Well, simmer down, Granny, we'll get it straightened out.

00:09:34.610 --> 00:09:39.570
Keep eye on the pools and stuff.

00:09:39.570 --> 00:09:41.320
- Guess what, Chief? - What?

00:09:41.320 --> 00:09:43.480
You're taking the Sea Witch tomorrow.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:44.650
I am not.

00:09:44.650 --> 00:09:46.950
You know she doesn't get along with the clappings.

00:09:46.950 --> 00:09:49.290
The Sea Witch is the boat you've chartered.

00:09:49.290 --> 00:09:52.760
Oh, well, don't scare me like that.

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:05.920
my doggies we come here to complain about having no place to fish you want to see where poor mr

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:21.590
drydale is trying his luck drydale you catching anything out of there well i i wasn't really

00:10:21.590 --> 00:10:30.000
trying to catch anything we was trying what did you get one old boot and it don't fit too good i thought the hunting around

00:10:30.000 --> 00:10:33.280
The pond here was poor, but the fishing is really miserable.

00:10:33.280 --> 00:10:36.960
We found a pond with some fish in it up the street yonder.

00:10:36.960 --> 00:10:40.400
The one with the spring and the bubbles coming out of it in the middle.

00:10:40.400 --> 00:10:43.200
Oh, that's the municipal fountain, and those are goldfish.

00:10:43.200 --> 00:10:44.980
I hope you didn't try to catch any of those.

00:10:44.980 --> 00:10:47.820
Oh, yeah, we caught a lot of them, but we threw them all back.

00:10:47.820 --> 00:10:50.580
The biggest one was no better than that.

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:54.560
Mr. Clavitt, Granny, tomorrow you will have the fishing experience of your lives.

00:10:54.560 --> 00:10:56.170
I have chartered a boat.

00:10:56.170 --> 00:10:58.200
I ain't setting foot on no boat.

00:10:58.200 --> 00:11:00.000
Granny, you'll be perfectly safe.

00:11:00.090 --> 00:11:04.770
large and there's an expert crew board. I ain't getting on no boat. But you love

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:10.060
it, Granny. Wait till you experience the thrill of hooking a giant barracuda. I ain't

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:17.380
going. A fighting sea bass. I ain't going. Bonita, albacore. I ain't going. Yellowtail.

00:11:17.380 --> 00:11:26.620
You can call me coward all you want. I ain't going. Please, Mr. Clappin, bring her back.

00:11:26.620 --> 00:11:30.000
I want to explain. I'll bring her back, but you can have a job

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:31.060
I'm getting her on the boat.

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:32.320
Granny's right fond of fishing,

00:11:32.320 --> 00:11:34.620
but she likes to do her rocking on dry land.

00:11:37.230 --> 00:11:38.390
- Doggone it.

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:40.370
Deep sea game fishing is made to order

00:11:40.370 --> 00:11:42.060
for that scrappy little woman.

00:11:42.060 --> 00:11:44.060
One look at a 500 pound sea bass

00:11:44.060 --> 00:11:46.220
and she'll never fish in fresh water again.

00:11:46.220 --> 00:11:47.860
- Chief, I've got an idea.

00:11:47.860 --> 00:11:49.040
An inspiration.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:50.360
It combines all the elements.

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:52.130
- Oh, wait a minute, wait a minute.

00:11:52.130 --> 00:11:55.170
Can you tell me in 5,000 words or less?

00:11:55.170 --> 00:11:58.790
- I can tell you in one word, marine land.

00:11:58.790 --> 00:12:00.000
- Marine land, yes.

00:12:00.640 --> 00:12:03.340
there with her feet firmly planted on dry land.

00:12:03.980 --> 00:12:07.320
Granny can gaze through glass windows into a huge tank

00:12:07.320 --> 00:12:10.540
and observe at arm's length specimens of sea life

00:12:10.540 --> 00:12:15.320
from the largest mammals to the tiniest crustaceans.

00:12:16.460 --> 00:12:19.140
Now, Granny, it won't hurt you then to listen to Mr. Drydee.

00:12:19.260 --> 00:12:21.280
He ain't getting me out on no water.

00:12:21.620 --> 00:12:24.020
But, Granny, I know how fond you are of fish.

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:25.960
I'm fond of ducks, too.

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:28.410
But I ain't going up in the sky after them.

00:12:29.330 --> 00:12:30.000
Sit down.

00:12:30.090 --> 00:12:39.660
Mr. Drysdale told us about a fishin' hole that's got 'em all beatin'

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:41.660
He's gonna take us out there first thing in the mornin'.

00:12:41.660 --> 00:12:42.660
It's a gymnastic.

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:45.660
You can look through a glass window and pick out the kind of fish you like.

00:12:45.660 --> 00:12:47.660
Some of 'em as big as this truck.

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:48.780
Woo!

00:12:48.780 --> 00:12:49.780
Let's go right now!

00:12:49.780 --> 00:12:50.780
Yeah!

00:12:50.780 --> 00:12:51.780
I'm all for that, Jack!

00:12:51.780 --> 00:12:55.180
Well, I reckon it would spare Mr. Drysdale a heap of trouble.

00:12:55.180 --> 00:12:56.770
Yeah, where is it, Paul?

00:12:56.770 --> 00:12:59.330
Down there in the ocean.

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:00.000
A place called Mr. Drysdale.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:01.460
It's called Marine Land.

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:03.460
Do you reckon the Marines would let us fish there?

00:13:03.460 --> 00:13:05.680
Well, he didn't say nothing about that.

00:13:05.680 --> 00:13:07.840
Well, they hadn't better try to stop me.

00:13:07.840 --> 00:13:10.720
I'm catching one of them whoppers for supper tonight.

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:12.640
The Marines is real tough fighters, Granny.

00:13:12.640 --> 00:13:13.830
And they tote guns.

00:13:13.830 --> 00:13:17.030
Well, let's stop by home and get our guns.

00:13:17.030 --> 00:13:17.870
Hold on, Granny.

00:13:17.870 --> 00:13:20.150
We ain't taking on the whole United States Marine.

00:13:20.150 --> 00:13:23.050
And he called me a yellowtail.

00:13:23.050 --> 00:13:24.750
Oh, you'll fight him, Granny.

00:13:24.750 --> 00:13:26.290
Ain't nobody gonna fight nobody.

00:13:26.290 --> 00:13:28.090
We'll ask them Marines nice and polite

00:13:28.090 --> 00:13:29.670
can we fish in a fishing hole.

00:13:30.030 --> 00:13:30.950
They say no.

00:13:30.950 --> 00:13:32.370
We'll jump that quick when we come to it.

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:33.250
Drive on, Jethro.

00:13:48.720 --> 00:14:00.000
No, sir, Air Force.

